WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.700
Think of your body as running two completely different supply chains depending on

2
00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:08.860
whether you have just eaten.

3
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:14.140
In the fed state, blood glucose is abundant, so cells simply burn it.

4
00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:17.800
Glycolysis feeds the Krebs cycle, which makes ATP.

5
00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:24.000
But hours after a meal, glucose runs scarce, and the brain and red blood cells still

6
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.040
demand it constantly.

7
00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:31.180
Now the body switches to gluconeogenesis, manufacturing glucose from non-sugar raw

8
00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:34.120
materials. Three streams feed that factory.

9
00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:39.920
Lactate from muscle and red cells, alanine from muscle protein, and glycerol from

10
00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:45.400
fat breakdown. Hold onto this fed versus fasted framing because it explains every

11
00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:47.380
regulatory decision we make today.

12
00:00:48.233 --> 00:00:53.753
Most of glycolysis is freely reversible, and gluconeogenesis simply runs those steps

13
00:00:53.753 --> 00:00:59.273
backward. The problem lives in exactly three places where glycolysis releases so

14
00:00:59.273 --> 00:01:03.213
much free energy that the reaction is effectively one way.

15
00:01:03.833 --> 00:01:10.413
Look at the numbers—hexokinase, phosphofructokinase, and pyruvate kinase—each have

16
00:01:10.413 --> 00:01:14.973
large negative delta ΔG values, so reversing them directly is impossible.

17
00:01:15.873 --> 00:01:20.853
Gluconeogenesis solves this with four dedicated bypass enzymes, shown in orange,

18
00:01:21.793 --> 00:01:27.613
glucose-6 -phosphatase, fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase, and the pyruvate

19
00:01:27.613 --> 00:01:31.313
carboxylase, plus PEP carboxykinase pair.

20
00:01:32.473 --> 00:01:37.173
Notice that pyruvate to PEP is split into two reactions, whose combined energetics

21
00:01:37.173 --> 00:01:38.293
make the climb feasible.

22
00:01:38.293 --> 00:01:41.533
Same map, different roads around the cliffs.

23
00:01:42.367 --> 00:01:45.187
Here is a logistics problem the cell must solve.

24
00:01:45.867 --> 00:01:51.387
Pyruvate carboxylase lives inside the mitochondrial matrix, so oxaloacetate is made

25
00:01:51.387 --> 00:01:57.407
there. But the next enzyme, PEP carboxykinase, needs that oxaloacetate out in the

26
00:01:57.407 --> 00:02:01.667
cytoplasm, and the membrane has no oxaloacetate transporter.

27
00:02:02.227 --> 00:02:03.687
The workaround is elegant.

28
00:02:04.207 --> 00:02:09.947
Reduce oxaloacetate to malate, which can cross, then reoxidize malate back to

29
00:02:09.947 --> 00:02:11.787
oxaloacetate on the other side.

30
00:02:11.787 --> 00:02:18.587
That second oxidation regenerates cytoplasmic NADH, which gluconeogenesis later

31
00:02:18.587 --> 00:02:24.207
needs anyway. So one shuttle accomplishes two jobs at once, moving carbon out and

32
00:02:24.207 --> 00:02:28.967
delivering reducing power exactly where the downstream reactions will consume it.

33
00:02:29.617 --> 00:02:34.617
Why does gluconeogenesis need its own enzymes instead of just reversing glycolysis?

34
00:02:35.037 --> 00:02:38.037
Think of it as a bargain the cell strikes with thermodynamics.

35
00:02:38.717 --> 00:02:44.717
Glycolysis releases energy, about 74 kilojoules per mole, so it is downhill and

36
00:02:44.717 --> 00:02:50.197
spontaneous. Gluconeogenesis consumes energy, and that extra cost, the six

37
00:02:50.197 --> 00:02:54.917
nucleotide triphosphate equivalents, is precisely what tips the synthetic direction

38
00:02:54.917 --> 00:02:56.217
to also be favorable.

39
00:02:56.757 --> 00:03:01.697
If synthesis were free, both pathways would run simultaneously in the same cell,

40
00:03:02.157 --> 00:03:07.357
creating a futile cycle that burns ATP and dumps it as heat with no net product.

41
00:03:08.057 --> 00:03:12.197
The energy cost is not waste, it is the price of directional control.
